# medium-scraper — copy to .env next to the scripts and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   python3 env_config.py        # prints what was picked up, WITHOUT secrets
#
# Credentials belong here and never on a command line: a secret in argv is
# readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in shell history.
# `.env` is in .gitignore; keep it that way.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#   an explicit flag  ->  an exported environment variable  ->  .env  ->  default
#
# Every value below is a PLACEHOLDER. Anything still carrying `{...}` braces
# is treated as unset, so a copied example is never sent to an API as if it
# were a key — the alternative is a 401 a long way from its cause.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2Captcha — one key, four separately billed products
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Captcha solving, the Scraping Browser API, proxies and fingerprints all sit
# behind this one key: https://2captcha.com
#
# Worth knowing before you buy any of it: on this site NONE of it is required
# to get data, and the captcha half of it cannot help at all.
#
# What Medium serves when it refuses is Cloudflare's MANAGED challenge, which
# publishes no sitekey — measured 0 `data-sitekey` attributes and 0 iframes
# on the interstitial, with `cType: 'managed'` in Cloudflare's own config.
# There is nothing to hand a solver, so this scraper never spends a solve on
# it and nothing is ever charged. The challenge clears on its own: 9 of 27
# first attempts were challenged and all 9 were served in full on the next
# attempt in a FRESH browser context.
#
# What a datacentre address with no key and no proxy actually got on
# 2026-09-16: HTTP 200 and the full page on 8 of 8 headful attempts, and 53
# to 128 stories per fetch.
#
# What the key DOES buy here, measured the same day:
#   * the Scraper API returned all four modes at $0.0005 a request, with rows
#     identical to a local browser's — including the 2.3 MB archive day in
#     ONE request, with no browser installed anywhere;
#   * the Scraping Browser returned 55 rows from an author page where a local
#     browser got 10, because that feed extends by scrolling and the scroll
#     is refused from an ordinary address and not from its exit.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Scraping Browser API — a remote browser over CDP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   country-{cc}   picks the exit country. Medium serves one catalogue in
#                  every language from one set of hosts, so this is a rate
#                  lever rather than a content one
#
# Measured over this endpoint on 2026-09-16: 254 rows across two archive
# days at 100% payload coverage, 60 from a tag feed, 55 from an author page
# (against a local browser's 10), 1 from a story page. A profile's
# credentials live about a day, so the shape below is documented and no
# working endpoint is ever pasted into this repo.
#   pid-{id}       a profile with persistent cookies, ONE live connection
#                  each — reuse pids rather than minting one per run
#
# --concurrency is refused with --cdp-endpoint (workers would collide on the
# profile); use several pids, one run each.
#
# NOTE: selenium_scraper.py cannot use this. chromedriver's `debuggerAddress`
# takes a bare host:port with nowhere to put a password; Playwright and
# pyppeteer authenticate on the WebSocket upgrade and work fine.
MEDIUM_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-us-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A proxy exit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read as a single exit, or use --proxy-file for a pool.
#
# What a proxy is and is NOT for on this site:
#
#  * It is NOT needed to get in. An ordinary datacentre address with no proxy
#    at all was served the full page 8 times out of 8 — see the note above.
#
#  * It DOES spread the rate, which is the thing that actually stops a long
#    run here. An archive walk pulls 2.3 MB per day URL, and one address went
#    from serving a whole day to the hard WAF refusal inside three of them.
#    --delay is the cheaper lever; a pool is the one that scales, and
#    --mode archive is the only mode where workers have anything to do.
#
# Note Selenium cannot authenticate a proxy at all (--proxy-server takes an
# address only). 2Captcha's IP-whitelist mode is the way around that.
MEDIUM_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@us.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A default URL, so a run needs no --url
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any of the four views:
#
#   tag       https://medium.com/tag/python
#             https://medium.com/tag/%E6%97%A5%E6%9C%AC%E8%AA%9E
#   archive   https://medium.com/tag/python/archive/2026/09/10
#   author    https://medium.com/@quincylarson
#   post      https://medium.com/p/d373fe2c96b7
#
# Prefer an ARCHIVE day when you want volume, reading times, word counts or
# languages: a day URL carries up to 128 stories with 84 fields each, where a
# tag feed carries 34 with 21 and no reading time at all. The `data_source`
# column on every row says which view built it.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix. `--out` already carries a
# non-empty default, so the loader would never see it as unset and the
# variable would be silently inert — a setting that looks configurable and is
# not.
MEDIUM_URL=https://medium.com/tag/python
